**Hypothesis (answer): Interviews.**

Interviews are the most commonly used requirements-elicitation technique for ML-enabled system
projects. Among the 194 respondents who answered the Q9 elicitation block (276 total; 82 skipped the
whole block), 97 reported using interviews = **50.0%, 95% bootstrap CI [42.8, 57.2]** (percentile
CI, row-level resampling with replacement, B=10,000, seed=42; B=100,000 gives the same interval).
Ranking of the named techniques (all on the same denominator, so the comparison is unbiased):
Interviews 50.0% [42.8, 57.2] > Scenarios = Prototyping 38.7% [32.0, 45.4] > Workshops/Meetings
38.1% [31.4, 44.8] > Observation 33.0% [26.3, 39.7] > Others 5.7% (11 mentions, descriptive only).
Bootstrapping supports the winner: Interviews stays strictly first in 97.3% of replicates
(one-sided p≈0.027) and its lead over each competitor has a CI excluding zero (+11.3 pp [1.5, 21.1]
vs Scenarios, +11.3 pp [2.0, 21.1] vs Prototyping, +11.9 pp [3.6, 20.6] vs Workshops/Meetings,
+17.0 pp [6.7, 27.3] vs Observation). The finding is invariant to the denominator choice (n=194
answered: 50.0%; n=276 all respondents: 35.1%; n=170 users of >=1 technique: 57.1%).

Note on the 69.399% (95% CI [69.062, 69.735]) quoted in the question: that figure is *not*
reproducible from the supplied file. Q9 here is coded as quoted / not quoted / -77 (no frequency
Likert), 69.399% of n=194 would require a non-integer 134.63 respondents (the only exact fractions
are 127/183, 254/366, ...), no statistic in the file matches 69.399%, and a ±0.34 pp interval
implies an effective sample ~100x larger than n=194 (observed/analytic SE ≈3.6 pp). So the
technique identity is robust and reproducible; the exact percentage and CI appear to come from a
differently coded version of this survey (or a non-standard CI formula).
